From ebd4990d045b1abd9fbd30e1d77f509a3849da02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 23:44:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] [Perf][Qwen4Exp] Let the dense W8A8 arm serve decode
 batches

Patch 12 floors the dense int8 arm at M=512, which leaves every captured
decode bucket on the bf16 GEMM. That floor came from the 2026-10-22 prefill
bench, and decode was never measured, so it was a guess about a regime the
shooter never entered.

Measured on 2026-10-23 the way decode actually runs, with each arm captured in
a CUDA graph and the replay timed, the arm beats the deployed bf16 GEMM at the
single-stream MTP-4 batch of 5 rows on all five large dense shapes: 1.47x on
in_proj_qkv, 1.47x in_proj_z, 1.50x out_proj, 1.42x q_proj, 1.19x o_proj.
Weighted over the 895 GB/s dense slice of the step that predicts roughly +13%
single-stream decode, before quality.

Eager timing inverts this result and says the arm loses on 4 of 6 shapes. The
extra per-token activation quant costs about 20 us of launch overhead that
graph replay removes, against a bf16 GEMM of 16 to 54 us, so the floor has to
be measured under capture. cuBLASLt int8 reaches 2.20-2.33x on the same shapes,
but vLLM exposes no small-M cuBLASLt int8 binding (torch._int_mm refuses
M<=16), so the CUTLASS arm is what ships.

VLLM_QWEN4EXP_DENSE_W8A8_MIN_M sets the floor and defaults to 512, so the
shipped behaviour is unchanged until it is set. Two shapes slip below parity
at the M=40 bucket (0.92x out_proj, 0.82x o_proj); the boot A/B decides whether
that matters against the M=5 and M=80 wins.
